  <result aggregation-status="0" type="2">
   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Output 2.4. Increased capacity Member States to apply sectoral international labour standards, codes of practice and guidelines</narrative>
   </title>
   <description>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">In 2023, the Government of Zimbabwe adopted an initiative to promote decent work in the cultural and creative industries in Zimbabwe in line with the ILO/UNESCO/WIPO International Convention for the Protection of Performers, Producers of Phonograms and Broadcasting Organisations (Rome Convention) of 1961 and the Transition from the Informal to the Formal Economy Recommendation, 2015 (No. 204): 
a.	A baseline survey was conducted to analyse the state of formalization in the creative industry in Zimbabwe covering sound recording and music publishing; programming and broadcasting; live music and theatre performances; motion pictures; video and television programme production; video games; writing and publishing; visual arts; crafts and design and fashion.
b.	Recommendations were developed to facilitate the transition to formality in selected sectors of the creative industry.
 The ILO provided financial support to undertake the study, and provided technical inputs to finalize the inception report, the National Action Plan as well as to prepare both the data collection tool and the informant interviews guide with collaboration from UNESCO.</narrative>
   </description>
